<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    Opt-in swipe counting (M7) and the Reels-only timer. What this service requests, and why:

    - canRetrieveWindowContent="true" and flagReportViewIds: the Reels-only timer has to tell
      Instagram Reels / TikTok For You from the other screens of those apps. It only does so after
      the user agreed to screen checks on the in-app disclosure (asked again for existing users),
      and only for Instagram and TikTok while "Show timer only in Reels" is on. It looks up only the
      view IDs listed in SectionRulesConfig (findAccessibilityNodeInfosByViewId) and reads, per node,
      viewIdResourceName, className, isSelected and isVisibleToUser, plus the window class of window
      changes. It never reads text, content descriptions, hints or anything the user sees or types
      (SectionPrivacyGuardTest checks the code); only the verdict (Reels or not) is kept, in memory.
      Swipe counting itself still uses only each event's type, package and scroll position numbers.
    - Events: scroll and window-state changes (swipes, the swipe limit), plus window-content and
      view-selected changes so a tab switch is noticed. The service turns the last two off at
      runtime (serviceInfo.eventTypes) while no app's screen is checked.
    - Only the tracked apps: packageNames lists the default apps (DefaultTrackedApps;
      AccessibilityConfigTest checks this) so the limit holds from the first event; the service
      replaces it with the user's tracked list (TrackedAppsRepository) when it connects and whenever
      the list changes, and drops events from any other app.

    flagIncludeNotImportantViews: full-screen video pagers (Instagram Reels, TikTok, YouTube Shorts)
    mark their scrolling view as "not important for accessibility", and Android drops events from
    such views for every service that doesn't set this flag. Without it, swipes in exactly the apps
    Unscroll is for never arrived. No other flag (no flagRetrieveInteractiveWindows).

    isAccessibilityTool="false": Unscroll is a digital-wellbeing app, not a tool that helps people
    with disabilities use their device (like a screen reader or switch access). Claiming otherwise
    would be inaccurate and against Google Play policy, so we declare it as false and complete the
    Accessibility API declaration instead (docs/ACCESSIBILITY_DECLARATION.md).
-->
<accessibility-service xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools"
    android:accessibilityEventTypes="typeViewScrolled|typeWindowStateChanged|typeWindowContentChanged|typeViewSelected"
    android:accessibilityFeedbackType="feedbackGeneric"
    android:accessibilityFlags="flagDefault|flagIncludeNotImportantViews|flagReportViewIds"
    android:canRetrieveWindowContent="true"
    android:description="@string/accessibility_service_description"
    android:isAccessibilityTool="false"
    android:notificationTimeout="100"
    android:packageNames="com.instagram.android,com.zhiliaoapp.musically,com.ss.android.ugc.trill,com.facebook.katana"
    android:settingsActivity="com.unscroll.app.MainActivity"
    tools:ignore="UnusedAttribute" />
